  "utmEpsg": 32616,
  "placementWhy": "Similarity fit on three points: the UTM point printed on the map (WGS84 16Q 467258E 2269789N, the map's own georeference, which is also the database's Sagitario position to the metre) at the centre of Sagitario's three openings, and the database's Pu'bix and Kilix Pach-Och (validity unknown, no source) at their entry arrows. Fit: 0.596 m/px (the 250 m scale bar: 415.7 px, 0.601 m/px, 0.9% apart), rotated -0.9 deg (the north arrow is up; UTM grid convergence there is about -0.1 deg); residuals Sagitario 1.0 m, Pu'bix 3.3 m, Kilix Pach-Och 2.3 m. A fit on Pu'bix and Kilix alone puts the printed UTM point between Sagitario's openings (0.601 m/px, 0.0 deg). The Fig. 1D profile (Sagitario to Pu'bix, about 1,310 m along the line) agrees. No ground imagery check was possible: Esri and Mapbox imagery show unbroken canopy (and cloud over Sagitario), and no other placed map is within 5 km. The database's three positions agree with the map within metres, so they likely come from the same CINDAQ survey: placed on the surveyors' printed georeference (user's decision, 2026-10-15).",
